TEXT 312 208 Left 2 ;Earth - Groundwire
TEXT 256 224 Left 2 ;Stroke
TEXT -296 152 Left 2 !.options vntol=1 abstol=1m method=gear reltol=0.01
TEXT 72 120 Left 5 ;R of transmission tower = 20ohm
TEXT -16 392 Left 2 ;Overhead line length only very small for not be confused\nby the transients of switching off and on of a longer line
TEXT 640 344 Left 2 ;Phase angle of the source2\nis 2 degrees to let flow\nactive power from source2 to \nsource1
TEXT 104 168 Left 2 ;two strokes
TEXT 48 -64 Left 2 ;This example shows two lightning strikes on the ground wire of a 110-kV overhead line. \nThe first lightning strike occurs at t=2 ms with a current of 100 kA.\nThe lightning current is conducted into the two towers and, due to the 20-ohm \nresistances to ground, generates a ground wire voltage of 1 megavolt.  \nThis high voltage creates an arc to phase 2 of the line. \nThis arc is fed on the one hand by the lightning current and on the other hand by the grid. \nThe lightning current decreases very rapidly to zero. \nWithout interrupting the two circuit breakers of the overhead line, the arc would be\nsustained by the grid. Starting at 5 ms, the line is disconnected from the grid and\nthe arc extinguishes shortly thereafter. \nThe line is switched back on at 20 ms. This simulates a brief interruption of the line\n(of course with switching times that are far too short to keep the simulation time\n brief).After the brief interruption, the line is back to normal and the arc has\nextinguished. \nAt 35 ms, a second arc strikes, and it is now shown that the arc persists due to the input of the grid\nThe line can be switched off later due to the persisting high current of the arc fault
TEXT 360 224 Left 2 ;Resistance of the transmission tower and grounding
TEXT -208 136 Left 2 ;method=gear for faster simulation !!
